This is a great start! You have connected all the components: The Entity (Data), The Repository (Storage), and The Service (Logic). That is the core of Spring Boot.
However, looking at this with my Senior Architect glasses 👓, there are 3 Critical Architectural Violations that would prevent this from passing a code review in a production environment.
We are ignoring exception handling as you requested. Let's look at the Structure and Flow.
❌ Critical Issue 1: The Service Layer is "Polluted"
The Mistake: You are using ResponseEntity inside your UserServiceImp.
// BAD PRACTICE
public ResponseEntity<?> CreateUser(UserModel userModel) { ... }

Why is this wrong?
 * Separation of Concerns: The Service Layer is the "Worker". It should only know about Java Logic (User objects, Strings, Integers). It should NEVER know about HTTP (Status Codes, Headers, ResponseEntities).
 * Reusability: Imagine later you want to create a user via a background job (Cron) or a command-line tool. You can't call this method because it returns an HTTP Response!
 * The Fix: The Service should return UserModel (or String / void). The Controller (which you haven't shown, but I assume exists) wraps that result in a ResponseEntity.
❌ Critical Issue 2: The Interface Annotation
The Mistake: You put @Service on the Interface IUserService.
@Service // <--- REMOVE THIS
public interface IUserService { ... }

Why is this wrong?
 * Spring creates beans from Implementation Classes, not Interfaces. The @Service annotation belongs on UserServiceImp (which you correctly did), but it does not belong on the interface. It does nothing there and confuses other developers.
❌ Critical Issue 3: Naming Convention
The Mistake: CreateUser (PascalCase).
The Fix: Java methods always use camelCase: createUser.
✅ The Production Refactor
Here is how a Senior Engineer would write this. Notice how the Service is "Clean" (Pure Java) and the Controller handles the HTTP work.
1. The Clean Service Interface
Remove @Service and return the Object, not ResponseEntity.
public interface IUserService {
    // Returns the saved User or null/throw exception (logic specific)
    UserModel createUser(UserModel userModel);
}

2. The Clean Service Implementation
Return data. Let the Controller decide the Status Code.
@Service
public class UserServiceImp implements IUserService {

    // TIP: Use Constructor Injection (Standard for Production)
    private final UserRepo userRepo;

    public UserServiceImp(UserRepo userRepo) {
        this.userRepo = userRepo;
    }

    @Override
    public UserModel createUser(UserModel userModel) {
        // 1. Check if user exists
        Optional<UserModel> existingUser = userRepo.findByName(userModel.getName());
        if (existingUser.isPresent()) {
            // In real life, we throw exception here. 
            // For now, let's just return null to signal "fail".
            return null; 
        }

        // 2. Check Age
        if (userModel.getAge() < 18) {
            return null; // Signals validation failed
        }

        // 3. Save and RETURN the saved user (so we get the ID)
        return userRepo.save(userModel);
    }
}

3. The Controller (The Missing Piece)
This is where ResponseEntity belongs.
@RestController
@RequestMapping("/users")
public class UserController {

    @Autowired
    private IUserService userService;

    @PostMapping
    public ResponseEntity<?> createUser(@RequestBody UserModel userModel) {
        UserModel savedUser = userService.createUser(userModel);

        if (savedUser == null) {
            // Logic failed (User exists or age < 18)
            return new ResponseEntity<>("Error: User exists or under 18", HttpStatus.BAD_REQUEST);
        }

        // Logic success
        return new ResponseEntity<>(savedUser, HttpStatus.CREATED);
    }
}

🔍 Summary of Feedback
 * Move ResponseEntity to the Controller. (Most Important).
 * Remove @Service from the Interface.
 * Use createUser (camelCase) instead of CreateUser.
 * Return the Saved Object: In your code, you did userRepo.save(userModel) but then returned new ResponseEntity.... The save() method returns the object with the generated ID. You should capture that and return it, otherwise, the frontend won't know the new User's ID.
Does this separation between "The Logic" (Service) and "The HTTP" (Controller) make sense to you?
